Q: Angular throws Error: NG0205: Injector has already been destroyed. error properties: Object({ code: 205 }) A: <p>I had the exact same NG0205 error in Angular 20 SSR (migrate from 17). The issue was multiple <code>provideHttpClient</code> calls being merged incorrectly.</p>
<h2>The Problem</h2>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// app.config.ts (shared)
export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [
    provideHttpClient(withInterceptors(interceptors)), // ← Has interceptors
    // ...
  ],
};

// app.config.server.ts
const serverConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [
    provideHttpClient(withFetch(), withInterceptors([])), // ← Empty interceptors
  ],
};

export const config = mergeApplicationConfig(appConfig, serverConfig);
</code></pre>
<p><strong><code>mergeApplicationConfig</code> concatenates providers</strong> — it doesn't replace them. Both <code>provideHttpClient</code> calls remained active, so interceptors ran during SSR. When HTTP responses arrived after the injector was destroyed → <strong>NG0205</strong>.</p>
<h2>The Fix</h2>
<p><strong>1. Remove <code>provideHttpClient</code> from shared config:</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// app.config.ts
export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [
    // Removed: provideHttpClient(withInterceptors(interceptors)),
    provideRouter(routes),
    provideClientHydration(),
    // ...
  ],
};
</code></pre>
<p><strong>2. Add browser-specific HTTP config:</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// main.ts
import { mergeApplicationConfig } from '@angular/core';
import { provideHttpClient, withInterceptors } from '@angular/common/http';

const browserConfig = {
  providers: [provideHttpClient(withInterceptors(interceptors))],
};

bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, mergeApplicationConfig(appConfig, browserConfig));
</code></pre>
<p><strong>3. Server config stays the same</strong> (now the only <code>provideHttpClient</code> on server):</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// app.config.server.ts - unchanged
provideHttpClient(withFetch(), withInterceptors([])) // No interceptors during SSR
</code></pre>

Q: PHP write objects inline A: <p>Yes, if you are used to javascript and doing {name:'harry', job:'dev'} then this question makes a lot of sense. Perhaps one day PHP will do it, just for brevity.<br />
I find the PHP syntax a slight bit verbose here.<br />
But I often do this:</p>
<pre><code>$o = (object)[];
$o-&gt;name = 'harry';
$o-&gt;job = 'dev';
print_r( $o );
</code></pre>
<p>Perhaps not relevant...</p>
<p>Update:<br />
Still the syntax is a bit clunky.<br />
Perhaps a simpler way is to define a variadic, and call it with named parameters:</p>
<pre><code>
function obj( ...$p ) { return (object)$p }

//now you can use:
$t = obj( name:'harry', car:'ford' );
</code></pre>
<p>output:</p>
<pre><code>stdClass Object
(
    [name] =&gt; harry
    [car] =&gt; ford
)
</code></pre>
